[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a quick‑start “cheat sheet” you can copy‑paste into a sandbox (CodeSandbox, StackBlitz, a local `index.html`, or a Vite/Next app) to see a **MapLibre map that talks to a free tile service that needs no API key**.  
It shows three common ways to get a style that works out‑of‑the‑box:

| Option | What you get | Where the tiles come from | Does it need a key? |
|--------|--------------|---------------------------|---------------------|
| **MapLibre demo style** | Full‑featured vector‑tile style (streets, labels, terrain, sky) | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/` (hosted by the MapLibre team) | **No** |
| **OpenFreeMap style** | Light‑weight OSM‑based style (similar to Mapbox “streets”) | `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/` (community‑run) | **No** |
| **DIY raster XYZ** | Minimal raster basemap (OSM PNG tiles) + any custom layers you add | `https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | **No** |

You can start with any of them; the first two are the easiest because they already include a **style JSON** that points at a vector‑tile source, so you don’t have to write a style yourself.

---

## 1️⃣ Install / import MapLibre

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm i maplibre-gl
# or, if you prefer a CDN, skip the install step and use the <script type="module"> snippet later.
```

```js
// ES‑module import (MapLibre v6+ – the only format now)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
```

**CDN version (no build step needed)**

```html
<link
  href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
  rel="stylesheet"
/>
<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  // …your map code goes here (see below) …
</script>
```

> **Note:** MapLibre v6 dropped the UMD bundle, so the `default` export (`import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`) no longer works. Use the namespace import (`* as maplibregl`) or import the specific class you need (`import {Map} from 'maplibre-gl'`).

---

## 2️⃣ Pick a style that **doesn’t use `mapbox://` URLs**

### 🎯 Option A – MapLibre demo style (the fastest test)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // <-- free, no token
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],       // lon, lat
  zoom: 12
});
```

*Why this works*: The style JSON hosted at `demotiles.maplibre.org` already contains a vector‑tile source that points at `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`. No token, no CORS headaches.

### 🎯 Option B – OpenFreeMap (another ready‑made style)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // free OSM‑based style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});
```

OpenFreeMap is a community‑funded OSM tile provider. The style uses vector tiles (`.pbf`) and a sprite sheet that are all publicly accessible.

### 🎯 Option C – Build a tiny raster XYZ style yourself

If you just need a quick raster basemap (e.g., for a prototype) you can skip a remote style JSON entirely and create a **bare‑bones style on the fly**:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // OSM raster tiles (no token)
      'osm-tiles': {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution:
          '<a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">© OpenStreetMap contributors</a>'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'osm-basemap',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'osm-tiles',
        minzoom: 0,
        maxzoom: 19
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});
```

> **Tip:** If you later want vector tiles (for label styling, 3‑D terrain, etc.) you can swap the `type: 'raster'` source for a `type: 'vector'` source that points at a public vector‑tile endpoint (e.g., `https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`). The rest of the style stays the same.

